                <h1 class="content-title">No-Code Automation: <span class="gradient-text">Flow Designer &amp; Workflows</span></h1>
                <p class="content-subtitle">
                    Automate complex enterprise business processes without writing scripts — understand triggers, actions, 
                    flow logic, drag-and-drop data pills, and multi-stage approval pipelines.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 1: What is Flow Designer -->
            <section class="content-section" id="what-is-flow">
                <h2>What is Flow Designer?</h2>
                <p>
                    Historically, automating multi-step enterprise workflows required writing complex JavaScript code across 
                    legacy Workflow Editors and script engines.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Flow Designer</strong> is ServiceNow's modern, natural-language automation environment that enables 
                    both non-technical administrators and professional developers to build end-to-end automations visually.
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Natural Language Assembly</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Workflows read like plain English sentences: 
                            <em>"When a high-priority incident is created, ask the On-Call Manager for approval, then create a task for Network Support."</em>
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Live Execution Tracking</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Every time a flow runs, Flow Designer captures a detailed execution log showing exactly which branch 
                            executed, how long each step took, and what data passed through each node.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 2: Three Pillars -->
            <section class="content-section" id="three-pillars">
                <h2>The 3 Core Pillars of Flow Designer</h2>
                <p>
                    Every automated process in Flow Designer is composed of three building blocks:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>1. Triggers (When should this run?)</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            • <strong>Record Triggers:</strong> Runs when a record is <em>Created</em>, <em>Updated</em>, or <em>Created or Updated</em>.
                            <br>• <strong>Scheduled Triggers:</strong> Runs at set intervals (e.g. every Monday at 8:00 AM).
                            <br>• <strong>Application Triggers:</strong> Inbound REST webhooks or Service Catalog requests.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>2. Actions (What should happen?)</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Pre-built operational steps:
                            <br>• <em>Ask for Approval</em>
                            <br>• <em>Create Task</em> or <em>Update Record</em>
                            <br>• <em>Send Email Notification</em>
                            <br>• <em>Look Up Records</em>
                            <br>• Integration spokes (Post to Slack, create Jira ticket).
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>3. Flow Logic (How should it branch?)</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Decision structures:
                            <br>• <em>If / Else If / Else</em> conditions.
                            <br>• <em>For Each</em> loops (processing arrays of records).
                            <br>• <em>Wait for Condition</em> (pausing until a field changes).
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 3: Data Pills -->
            <section class="content-section" id="data-pills">
                <h2>Data Pills &amp; Visual Drag-and-Drop Mapping</h2>
                <p>
                    How do you pass data from step 1 into step 4? Through <strong>Data Pills</strong>:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>What is a Data Pill?</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            A visual pill representing a variable output from any previous step in the flow. 
                            For example: <code>[1. Trigger - Incident &gt; Caller &gt; Manager &gt; Email]</code>.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Zero Code Mapping</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Instead of writing string concatenations in JavaScript, you drag the <em>Caller Name</em> data pill 
                            directly into the <em>To</em> box of an email notification step!
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 4: Case Study -->
            <section class="content-section" id="case-study">
                <h2>Step-by-Step Case Study: VIP Hardware Request Flow</h2>
                <p>
                    Let's trace a real-world enterprise approval and fulfillment flow built in Flow Designer:
                </p>

                <div class="diagram-block">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Visual Automation Pipeline in Flow Designer</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    graph TD
                        TRG["1. Trigger: Service Catalog Request submitted<br>Item = Executive Laptop Package"] --> LKP["2. Look Up Record: Fetch Requester's Department Budget"]
                        
                        LKP --> COND{"3. Flow Logic: Is Cost > $2,000?"}
                        COND -->|Yes| APPR["4. Ask for Approval: Send notification to Department VP"]
                        COND -->|No| AUTO["Skip Approval: Auto-approve standard order"]
                        
                        APPR --> WAIT{"Approval Decision"}
                        WAIT -->|Approved| TASK["5. Create Catalog Task (SCTASK): Dispatch to IT Procurement"]
                        WAIT -->|Rejected| REJ["Send Rejection Email to Employee &amp; Close Request"]
                        
                        AUTO --> TASK
                        TASK --> CLOSE["6. Wait for SCTASK completion $\rightarrow$ Mark Request Closed Complete"]
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 5: Flow Designer vs Business Rules -->
            <section class="content-section" id="flow-vs-br">
                <h2>When to Use Flow Designer vs. Business Rules</h2>
                <p>
                    A common question for new ServiceNow developers is knowing when to build a Flow vs. writing a Business Rule:
                </p>

                <table class="data-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Scenario</th>
                            <th>Best Choice</th>
                            <th>Why</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Multi-day approval workflow with email reminders.</td>
                            <td><strong>Flow Designer</strong></td>
                            <td>Flows can pause, wait for days, and resume cleanly without tying up server threads.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Validating field values and stopping invalid saves before SQL commit.</td>
                            <td><strong>Business Rule (`before`)</strong></td>
                            <td>Business Rules run in milliseconds synchronously inside the database transaction.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Fulfilling Service Catalog order items across multiple teams.</td>
                            <td><strong>Flow Designer</strong></td>
                            <td>Built-in catalog item triggers, sequential task generation, and stage visualizers.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>High-frequency math calculation on every single record update.</td>
                            <td><strong>Business Rule (`before`)</strong></td>
                            <td>Zero execution overhead for instant mathematical field population.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

